From a28c8d4d276bbb600118977231ebc39946d24b9c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?St=C3=A9phane=20Payrard?= <cognominal@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:59:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] feat: hover help explains the dock and the desktop

Ctrl+b h (toggle_hover_help) turns on hover help, and Ctrl+b h turns it
off. The explainer is a borderless overlay on the theme's panel ground,
drawn above or below its target and never over it. It says what the
target is for, what it can show, its keys and the CLI that changes it.

The pointer picks the target; the help keys walk without it. They are
all modified, rebindable in [keybindings.help], and the footer shows
the keys the config binds:

  help_next / help_prev          ctrl+alt+right / ctrl+alt+left
  help_deeper / help_shallower   ctrl+alt+down / ctrl+alt+up
  help_scroll_up / _down         ctrl+alt+pgup / ctrl+alt+pgdown
  help_keys                      alt+shift+h

The help keys are read ahead of every other route while the explainer
is up, so no default is a key another table binds. A test presses
every section's default keys with hover help up, and another checks
the help defaults against every table, the GUI client's included.

Hover help is a view, not a mode: every other key reaches the pane,
clicks reach what they land on, and the wheel scrolls the explainer only
over it.

This first part explains the dock, each cell it drew, and the desktop.
The dock records one hit per named cell as it draws, so the explainer
places itself against the cells on screen.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
